uld  otherwise yeah you won't have that  balance or that feel for it maybe the  grip and if you do also train the bench  press then you are going to be able to  bring that strength over and apply it  building that mule drive through the  kind of overcoming isometric methods you  might see that this can translate to  better more powerful lifts and this is  where I struggle a little bit to  understand a lot of people's reasoning  because the people who say that all you  need are those big three lifts you know  bench press squats and deadlift will  also look down their nose at resistance  machines as I've said in the past the  bench press has actually applied to very  much in the real world because when you  lie on the ground and then press  something over the head you might have  that pushing strength but if you fly  backwards because you're not that heavy  well then it's not going to be all that  much use now especially if you're you  know arching your back puffing your  chest out and retracting your shoulder  blades who pushes like that nobody this  is not a practical exercise directly  and with the deadlift that's probably  the most function of the big three lifts  but even then you don't pick things up  in a direct line using perfect form  using a perfectly straight bar we pick  things up whilst twisting we pick things  up whilst moving and so we need more  variation if we want to cover every  single aspect of our movement  this is why I think it's important to do  things like twisting and moving in the  frontal plane so don't say that you  can't get any benefit from resistance  machines because the benefit you get  from resistance machines is just one  step back from the benefit that you  would get from those big three lifts no  movement perfectly matches everything  that you're going to do but by doing a  wide range of things we get a lot of  those different benefits and we combine  them in a useful way we learn the  movement patterns we train the  supporting mu